    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # All the Pencils! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Hold Up Ten Fingers!</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> All the Pencils!</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Respond nonverbally to simple yes/no questions by nodding or shaking head.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can count up to ten out loud with teacher guidance (e.g., saying the numbers up to ten using a number chart). Can name familiar objects while counting (e.g., 'five pencils'). Can respond to questions related to numbers (e.g., responding to the prompt, 'Is this the number five?' with 'yes' or 'no').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Count Our Fingers" song (e.g., "One, two, three, four, five, I have five fingers on this hand! Six, seven, eight, nine, ten, I have ten fingers!"). Students sing along and hold up fingers as they count. The teacher shows flashcards of numbers 1-10 and various colored pencils (red, yellow, blue, green, orange, purple) from the theme "All the Pencils!", asking, "What color is this pencil?" and "What number is this?". This activates prior knowledge of vocabulary and numbers from previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up their hand and slowly counts their fingers, saying, "One finger, two fingers, three fingers..." and emphasizing the initial /f/ sound in 'fingers' and /k/ in 'count'. Then, the teacher holds up a number flashcard (e.g., '5') and five pencils, counts them, and asks, "Is this number five?" while nodding. Then, the teacher holds up a different number flashcard (e.g., '3') with five pencils and asks, "Is this number five?" while shaking their head. The teacher demonstrates the nonverbal responses (nodding for 'yes', shaking head for 'no') for students to imitate. Students practice identifying numbers and counting objects using realia like pencils, crayons, and blocks. The teacher models the question 'How many fingers?' and answers 'Ten fingers!' with a clear voice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to number 7 on a chart) "Is this number seven?" (Expected: Students nod 'yes' or say 'yes').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up two blocks) "Are these five blocks?" (Expected: Students shake head 'no' or say 'no').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated "Counting Story" on large flashcards or a projector, featuring a child counting various school supplies (pencils, crayons, books, blocks, markers, glue) and their fingers. The story includes simple dialogue with questions like "How many pencils do you see?", "Is this number four?", and responses showing counting up to ten. The images clearly depict the quantities and numbers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student will be able to interpret and respond to simple questions about quantity and identification of numbers 1-10 in a visual context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> After showing the story, the teacher asks, "What is the boy doing?" (Expected: Counting) and "What is he counting?" (Expected: Pencils, fingers, etc.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (nonverbal response):</b> The teacher presents a flashcard from the story (e.g., 6 pencils) and states, "There are five pencils." Students nod for true or shake their heads for false. Repeat with different quantities and objects. Example: Teacher shows 3 books and asks, "Are there three books?" Students nod. Teacher shows 3 books and asks, "Are there ten books?" Students shake their heads.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using quantity + nouns (e.g., 'four pencils', 'eight fingers') and responding to Yes/No questions (e.g., 'Is this number seven?'). Focus on pronunciation of initial /k/ in 'count' and /f/ in 'fingers', and recognizing number sounds (1-10).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Show Me the Number!":</b> The teacher holds up number flashcards (1-10). Students look at the flashcard and count out the corresponding number of realia objects (pencils, blocks, crayons) at their desks, then hold them up. The teacher asks, "How many pencils?" and students chorally respond, e.g., "Five pencils!" or "Two blocks!". The teacher reinforces pronunciation of 'count' and 'fingers'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Substitution Drill – "Is This My Number?":</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with a number (e.g., '6') and then displays a group of objects that *may or may not* match (e.g., 6 pencils, then 4 pencils). The teacher asks, "Is this number six?" Students respond nonverbally (nod for yes, shake head for no). The teacher then asks a student to say "Yes!" or "No!". The activity rotates, encouraging all students to practice both nonverbal and verbal responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Count and Ask!":</b> Students work in pairs with a small collection of objects (e.g., 5 pencils, 3 crayons, 2 books, 1 glue). Student A picks an object and counts them aloud, e.g., "One, two, three, four, five pencils." Student B then asks, "Are there five pencils?" Student A responds (nodding/shaking head and saying "Yes" or "No"). They take turns, also practicing "What number is this?" with number flashcards. Teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation, encouraging turn-taking and active listening.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group "Counting Detective" Game.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a tray with mixed school supplies (e.g., a few red pencils, some blue crayons, a book, some blocks, some markers) and a set of number flashcards (1-10). One student is the "Detective." The Detective secretly picks a number flashcard and counts out that many objects from the tray (e.g., picks '7' and counts out seven orange pencils). The other students in the group ask the Detective questions: "How many pencils?" or "Is this number seven?" The Detective responds verbally (e.g., "Seven pencils!" or "Yes, it's seven!") and nonverbally (nod/shake head for yes/no questions). Students take turns being the Detective. The teacher observes groups, facilitating communication and ensuring all students have a chance to speak and respond, using the target vocabulary and grammatical structures (Quantity + nouns, Wh-questions, Yes/No questions).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Counting Detective" game and a quick individual check.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individual "Mystery Number Bag" check. Each student receives a small bag with a secret number of objects (e.g., 3 blocks, 7 crayons). The teacher asks each student individually, "How many [object] do you have?" and "Is this number [teacher says a number that may or may not be correct]?" The student counts the objects, responds verbally with the quantity, and nonverbally (nod/shake head) to the Yes/No question.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of numbers and target vocabulary (e.g., 'pencil', 'finger', 'count').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of quantity + nouns (e.g., "Three blocks").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct nonverbal response (nod/shake head) to Yes/No questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active participation and turn-taking in group activities.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up thumbs: "Thumb up if you can count to ten!" "Thumb up if you can answer 'How many?'" "Thumb up if you can say 'Yes' or 'No' to questions about numbers!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite thing to count today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me your fingers and count them to ten?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The students showed good engagement with the counting activities, especially with the realia. The nonverbal responses (nodding/shaking head) were largely successful, indicating good comprehension. Some students still need more practice articulating the full phrase 'quantity + noun' and consistently differentiating between similar number sounds, particularly 6 and 7, or 9 and 10. The initial /k/ and /f/ sounds need continued reinforcement. For the next lesson, I will incorporate more specific drills on tricky number sounds and ensure structured opportunities for all students to speak individually while counting to ten.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson will focus on Writing Composition, building on the counting and naming of objects by having students write numbers and simple quantity statements (e.g., "I see five pencils.") using provided sentence frames and visual aids.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>